            <div xml:id="d46">

                <pb facs="0037" n="33" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg33"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">600.939/227: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-23T15:00:00+08:00">July 23, 1938—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 23—11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>464. Embassy’s 456, July 20, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="52" xml:id="d46-f1"/> and
                    Tientsin’s 137, July 22, 3 p.m. A secretary of the Japanese Embassy called this
                    morning and stated informally that embargo will be lifted from most materials
                    not urgently needed by the military, including lambskin plates, lambear plates,
                    lambhead plates, and lambleg plates and all kinds of goat and kid skins but that
                    the embargo continues for the time being on tanned and untanned lambskins. He
                    stated that this means the embargo is lifted on almost all kinds of materials
                    heretofore exported to the United States. He expects detailed and official
                    information will be available in 2 or 3 days.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Embassies Hankow, Tokyo and Consulate Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
